-- D2's execution program: the resolved, replayable artifact.
--
-- WI-A13, stage 1. This module holds the TYPES and the PURE STRUCTURAL
-- VALIDATOR that D2 requires to run before replay. It holds no generator, no
-- driver, and no replay loop; those are later stages and later modules, so that
-- this one can be read and mutated without a world in scope.
--
-- Stage 2 moved the interaction vocabulary itself — `IdentityBody`,
-- `CausalIdentity`, `TimedOutcome`, `Interaction` and their derivations — down
-- into `src/core/dst_interaction`, which imports nothing but std. The reason is
-- structural and is recorded in that module's header: the port layer has to
-- name `Interaction` to carry the recorded log, and it cannot import this
-- module without dragging `ExecutionManifest` and the whole `dst_profile`
-- closure into the production driver's import graph. Stage 1's design notes 1
-- and 2 (identity-as-a-sum, ordinal-as-the-duplicate-key) travelled with the
-- types and are stated there.
--
-- One design decision is left here, because it is about the PROGRAM and not
-- about an interaction:
--
-- THE PROGRAM DOES NOT RESTATE THE WORLD'S CURSORS. `InitialWorld` carries
-- the synthetic environment, the clock epoch, and the normalized message and
-- policy inputs — but NOT the provider script, the approval queue, or the
-- tool queue. Those are projections of the interaction list
-- (stage 3 builds `world_state_of`). If the program carried both, a program
-- could serve a response that no interaction records, which is the exact
-- shape of the completeness defect this item exists to prevent.

module src/core/dst_program

import std/list as List (length)
import std/string as Str (length)
import std/option (Option, Some, None)
import src/core/dst_profile (ExecutionManifest)
import src/core/dst_generator (GeneratorBounds)
-- WI-D22. `InitialWorld.files` is kinded, and `FsNode` lives in a module that
-- imports std and nothing else precisely so that THIS module can name it without
-- importing the port layer. See `src/core/fs_node`'s header.
import src/core/fs_node (FsNode, FsFile, FsDir)
import src/core/dst_interaction (
  IdentityBody,
  ProviderIdentity, ToolIdentity, ApprovalIdentity, ExtensionEffectIdentity,
  EnvironmentReadIdentity, RandomDrawIdentity, ClockAdvanceIdentity,
  FileWriteIdentity, FileRemoveIdentity, DirMakeIdentity,
  CausalIdentity,
  StreamChunkRecord,
  OutcomeStatus, OutcomeOk, OutcomeFault, OutcomeMissing,
  TimedOutcome,
  Interaction,
  identity_kind,
  interaction_kind,
  class_has_deadline,
  class_may_stream
)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Schema version and the D8 compatibility policy
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- The program schema this build writes and can decode. D8 forbids silently
-- reinterpreting an old program: a version this build does not know is a
-- REJECTION carrying a pinned-runner pointer, never a best-effort decode.
-- WI-D22 MOVED THIS TO `execution-program/2`. Three payloads travel with the
-- move and each of them, added WITHOUT moving the version, would have been a
-- silent reinterpretation rather than a migration:
--
--   `InitialWorld.files`      owed since WI-D17 and grown a field by WI-D18. Its
--                             absence is why `world_state_of` reconstitutes
--                             `files: []` and a deterministic run's filesystem
--                             cannot be seeded at all.
--   `ScriptedTool.exit_code`  owed since WI-D21. Its absence is why the
--                             deterministic half cannot say whether a subprocess
--                             succeeded. See `ports.decode_tool_outcome` for the
--                             exact failure a version-less addition produces.
--   a FROZEN v2 SPECIMEN      owed since WI-D17 §7b. Three identity classes were
--                             round-tripped and frozen by nothing, because there
--                             were no historical bytes containing them. There
--                             are now: `scripts/dst/fixtures/execution-program-v2.artifact`.
--
-- WHAT AN ARTIFACT THAT PREDATES EACH FIELD DECODES TO, stated per field because
-- that is the question a migration has to answer and a widening never asks:
--
--   `world.file` lines        ABSENT in v1, and absence decodes to `files: []`.
--                             That is a DEFAULT and it is the correct one, for a
--                             reason that does not generalise: at v1 the field
--                             did not exist anywhere, so `world_state_of`
--                             unconditionally reconstituted `[]` and an empty
--                             table is what a v1 program MEANT. The decoder
--                             reproduces v1's semantics rather than guessing.
--   `exit_code`               ABSENT in v1, and absence is a REFUSAL, because
--                             here the two differ: v1 tool outcomes were
--                             produced by runs where a subprocess really did
--                             exit with something, and every default that is a
--                             valid status claims to know what. The rule is
--                             not "old fields default" or "old fields refuse" —
--                             it is that a default is admissible exactly when
--                             the old version's own semantics fixed the value,
--                             and refusal is required when they did not.
export pure func program_schema_version() -> string { "execution-program/2" }

-- Every schema version this build can decode. A migration extends this list
-- and keeps the old decode path, or it does not extend it and old programs
-- fail closed. There is no third option in which the program is read anyway.
--
-- THIS LIST WAS A ONE-ELEMENT LIST DERIVED FROM `program_schema_version()` AND
-- THAT WAS A TRAP, sprung by the first bump: moving the version alone silently
-- removed `execution-program/1` from the decodable set, and the build instantly
-- could not read `scripts/dst/fixtures/execution-program-v1.artifact` — the one
-- artifact in this project that predates the current encoder and the only thing
-- the compatibility policy is made of. `scenario_the_frozen_v1_specimen_still_decodes`
-- is the row that catches it.
--
-- Written out oldest-first rather than derived, so that a future bump has to
-- make the same decision explicitly instead of inheriting it. The versions this
-- build DECODES and the version it WRITES are two different facts and this list
-- is the first; `test_current_schema_is_decodable_and_an_unknown_one_is_not`
-- pins the relationship between them.
export pure func decodable_schema_versions() -> [string] {
  ["execution-program/1", program_schema_version()]
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The initial world and the generator's declared bounds
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- D2's `initial_world`. See design note 3 in the module header for why the
-- provider script, approval queue and tool queue are ABSENT: they are
-- projections of the interaction list, so a response the program serves is
-- necessarily a response the program records.
-- WI-D22 ADDED `files`, AND IT IS NOT A COUNTEREXAMPLE TO DESIGN NOTE 3. The
-- note keeps the provider script, the approval queue and the tool queue OFF the
-- program because they are projections of the interaction list — a program
-- carrying both could serve a response no interaction records. The file table is
-- the opposite kind of thing, and it is the same kind as
-- `synthetic_environment`, which has always been here: it is an INPUT the run
-- reads and does not produce. WI-D17 recorded the consequence of its absence
-- explicitly — `world_state_of` reconstitutes `files: []`, so the two filesystem
-- classes are "recorded for grading and not for reconstitution" and a
-- deterministic run's filesystem cannot be seeded at all.
--
-- IT IS KINDED, and that is forced rather than chosen: WI-D18 made
-- `WorldState.files` a `[{ path, node: FsNode }]` so that an empty directory is
-- representable, and a seed that cannot express a directory cannot seed the
-- world WI-D18 built. The alternative — a structurally identical
-- `[{ path, kind: string, content }]` here, converted at the boundary — was
-- available and is declined at `src/core/fs_node`'s header: it makes
-- `{ path, kind: "dir", content: "hello" }` representable at the PERSISTENCE
-- boundary, which is the one place hand-written and corrupted inputs enter.
export type InitialWorld = {
  messages_and_policy: string,
  synthetic_environment: [{ key: string, value: string }],
  files: [{ path: string, node: FsNode }],
  clock_epoch: int,
  extension_profile: string
}

-- D2: "Each generator schema declares hard bounds for interactions, stream
-- chunks, payload bytes, logical-resource size, and clock advancement.
-- Exceeding a bound is a generator/harness failure, not an unbounded test run."
--
-- Recorded ON the program, not only on the config, so that a replay of an
-- archived program can state the bounds it was generated under without needing
-- the config that produced it.
--
-- WI-A13 stage 4 MOVED the declaration down into `src/core/dst_generator`, for
-- the same structural reason stage 2 moved `Interaction` into
-- `src/core/dst_interaction`: the bounds are consulted at the moment of a
-- choice, which is inside a port adapter, and `ports.ail` cannot import this
-- module without dragging the whole `dst_profile` closure into the production
-- driver's import graph. It was re-exported here, as `type GeneratorBounds =
-- GeneratorBounds`, so that a reader still found it beside the program's other
-- types and so that no existing importer changed.
--
-- WI-B2a REMOVED that re-export, because AILANG v0.33.0 rejects it. The form is
-- accepted at its declaration and then resolves to ITSELF rather than to the
-- imported type, so the alias is unexpandable: any attempt to unify a record
-- literal against a field of this type fails with `cannot unify record with
-- unexpandable type constructor GeneratorBounds`. That took out `dst_replay`
-- and `execution_program_dst` — 8 files — because `ExecutionProgram.bounds`
-- carries the type. The declaration itself never errors, so the failure lands
-- at every CONSTRUCTION site and never at the cause.
--
-- The name is now imported (line 35) for this module's own use and NOT
-- re-exported; the two importers that took it from here now take it from
-- `src/core/dst_generator` directly.
--
-- `stub_step.ail:49` carried the identical form for `ScriptedStep` and was
-- removed in the same item, for the same reason. It was NOT a second, harmless
-- instance: it was blocking 33 files, invisibly, because they all died on an
-- effect row first. Both re-exports are records, and a record is exactly what
-- this defect eats — the alias only fails where something asks the compiler to
-- EXPAND it to its fields, which is every construction site and no declaration
-- site. If a third `export type X = X` appears, it is a defect wherever X is a
-- record, whether or not the tree is currently green.

export type DiscoveryConfig = {
  schema_version: string,
  generator_id: string,
  generator_version: string,
  seed: int,
  initial_world: InitialWorld,
  bounds: GeneratorBounds
}

-- D2's `ExecutionProgram`, with D8's requirement that the exact execution
-- manifest travel WITH the program rather than beside it. D11 states the
-- consequence: "a program promoted without one is not a reproduction unit — it
-- is a program nobody can state the meaning of." Making it a field rather than
-- a sidecar is what makes that structurally true.
export type ExecutionProgram = {
  schema_version: string,
  generator_id: string,
  generator_version: string,
  seed: int,
  initial_world: InitialWorld,
  bounds: GeneratorBounds,
  manifest: ExecutionManifest,
  interactions: [Interaction]
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Structural rejections
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- D2: program decoding and a pure structural validator "reject malformed
-- schemas, negative time, duplicate interaction identities, impossible static
-- references, and absent required initial values". Each constructor below is
-- one of those five, made specific enough to name the offending position.
export type ProgramRejection
  = SchemaFieldMissing(string)                  -- field
  | SchemaVersionUndecodable(string)            -- version seen
  | InitialValueMissing(string)                 -- field
  | NegativeTime(int, string, int)              -- ordinal, what, value
  | DuplicateOrdinal(int)                       -- the repeated ordinal
  | OrdinalNotDense(int, int)                   -- expected, found
  | ImpossibleReference(int, string)            -- ordinal, what
  | IdentityComponentMissing(int, string)       -- ordinal, component
  | DeadlineDisagreesWithClass(int, string)     -- ordinal, why
  | StreamOnNonProvider(int)                    -- ordinal
  | ChunkOffsetsNotMonotone(int)                -- ordinal
  | OutcomeStatusDisagreesWithFault(int, string) -- ordinal, why
  | EmptyProgram

export pure func program_rule(r: ProgramRejection) -> string {
  match r {
    SchemaFieldMissing(_) => "program-schema-field-missing",
    SchemaVersionUndecodable(_) => "program-schema-undecodable",
    InitialValueMissing(_) => "program-initial-value-missing",
    NegativeTime(_, _, _) => "program-negative-time",
    DuplicateOrdinal(_) => "program-duplicate-ordinal",
    OrdinalNotDense(_, _) => "program-ordinal-not-dense",
    ImpossibleReference(_, _) => "program-impossible-reference",
    IdentityComponentMissing(_, _) => "program-identity-component-missing",
    DeadlineDisagreesWithClass(_, _) => "program-deadline-disagrees-with-class",
    StreamOnNonProvider(_) => "program-stream-on-non-provider",
    ChunkOffsetsNotMonotone(_) => "program-chunk-offsets-not-monotone",
    OutcomeStatusDisagreesWithFault(_, _) => "program-outcome-status-disagrees-with-fault",
    EmptyProgram => "program-empty"
  }
}

export pure func program_message(r: ProgramRejection) -> string {
  match r {
    SchemaFieldMissing(f) =>
      "[program-schema-field-missing] the program has no '${f}'; D8 requires the schema version, generator id/family, generator version and seed to travel with every preserved failure, and a blank axis is an unexplainable program",
    SchemaVersionUndecodable(v) =>
      "[program-schema-undecodable] this build cannot decode program schema '${v}' and will NOT reinterpret it; D8 forbids silently reinterpreting an old program, so replay it with the pinned runner for that schema version (see decodable_schema_versions())",
    InitialValueMissing(f) =>
      "[program-initial-value-missing] the initial world has no '${f}'; D2 requires the validator to reject absent required initial values, because a world that starts undefined replays to whatever the runner happens to default to",
    NegativeTime(ord, what, v) =>
      "[program-negative-time] interaction #${ord} records ${what} = ${v}; D4's clock is monotonic and D2 requires a non-negative recorded advance, so negative time is a malformed program rather than a fast run",
    DuplicateOrdinal(ord) =>
      "[program-duplicate-ordinal] two interactions carry encounter ordinal ${ord}; the ordinal is the global position and cannot be shared. Note this rule is deliberately blind to the identity BODY: a repeated production call id is representable on purpose, so an invariant can report it as system behaviour instead of the decoder rejecting the artifact (D2)",
    OrdinalNotDense(expected, found) =>
      "[program-ordinal-not-dense] expected encounter ordinal ${expected} at this position but found ${found}; ordinals are the global encounter order, so a gap means an interaction was dropped from the program and the log no longer accounts for the execution",
    ImpossibleReference(ord, what) =>
      "[program-impossible-reference] interaction #${ord} ${what}; D2 requires the validator to reject impossible static references, and a class id no catalogue defines cannot be counted by D11's class-reached counter",
    IdentityComponentMissing(ord, c) =>
      "[program-identity-component-missing] interaction #${ord} has a blank '${c}'; D2 fixes the identity components per class, and a blank one makes two different logical requests compare equal during replay",
    DeadlineDisagreesWithClass(ord, why) =>
      "[program-deadline-disagrees-with-class] interaction #${ord} ${why}; D2 gives a deadline to ExpectTool, ExpectApproval and ExpectExtensionEffect and to no other class",
    StreamOnNonProvider(ord) =>
      "[program-stream-on-non-provider] interaction #${ord} carries stream chunks but is not a provider interaction; D2 puts ordered chunks on the provider timed outcome only",
    ChunkOffsetsNotMonotone(ord) =>
      "[program-chunk-offsets-not-monotone] interaction #${ord} has stream chunks whose virtual offsets decrease; D2 requires non-decreasing offsets, and a decreasing offset would replay chunks in an order the driver never observed",
    OutcomeStatusDisagreesWithFault(ord, why) =>
      "[program-outcome-status-disagrees-with-fault] interaction #${ord} ${why}; a fault with no class id is uncountable by D11's class-reached counter and an ok outcome naming one inflates it",
    EmptyProgram =>
      "[program-empty] the program records no interactions; an execution that requested nothing is not a reproduction of a run, and a replay of it would assert nothing at all"
  }
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The validator
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

pure func blank(s: string) -> bool { s == "" }

pure func contains_str(xs: [string], target: string) -> bool {
  match xs {
    [] => false,
    x :: rest => if x == target then true else contains_str(rest, target)
  }
}

pure func contains_int(xs: [int], target: int) -> bool {
  match xs {
    [] => false,
    x :: rest => if x == target then true else contains_int(rest, target)
  }
}

pure func require_field(s: string, field: string) -> [ProgramRejection] {
  if blank(s) then [SchemaFieldMissing(field)] else []
}

pure func require_initial(s: string, field: string) -> [ProgramRejection] {
  if blank(s) then [InitialValueMissing(field)] else []
}

-- Schema envelope. The undecodable-version check is separate from the
-- blank-field check so that an old program produces D8's pinned-runner pointer
-- rather than a generic "malformed" message that invites a retry.
pure func validate_schema(p: ExecutionProgram) -> [ProgramRejection] {
  require_field(p.schema_version, "schema_version")
    ++ require_field(p.generator_id, "generator_id")
    ++ require_field(p.generator_version, "generator_version")
    ++ (if blank(p.schema_version) || contains_str(decodable_schema_versions(), p.schema_version)
        then []
        else [SchemaVersionUndecodable(p.schema_version)])
}

pure func validate_initial_world(w: InitialWorld) -> [ProgramRejection] {
  require_initial(w.messages_and_policy, "messages_and_policy")
    ++ require_initial(w.extension_profile, "extension_profile")
    ++ (if w.clock_epoch < 0 then [NegativeTime(-1, "initial_world.clock_epoch", w.clock_epoch)] else [])
}

-- Per-class identity component discipline (D2's per-class identity fields).
-- The extension-effect call id is exempt: D2 says "call id where the effect has
-- one", so blank is legitimate there and only there.
pure func validate_identity(id: CausalIdentity) -> [ProgramRejection] {
  let o = id.ordinal;
  match id.body {
    ProviderIdentity(origin, step, model) =>
      (if blank(origin) then [IdentityComponentMissing(o, "provider origin")] else [])
        ++ (if blank(model) then [IdentityComponentMissing(o, "provider model")] else [])
        ++ (if step < 0 then [NegativeTime(o, "provider step", step)] else []),
    ToolIdentity(origin, call_id, name) =>
      (if blank(origin) then [IdentityComponentMissing(o, "tool origin")] else [])
        ++ (if blank(call_id) then [IdentityComponentMissing(o, "tool call_id")] else [])
        ++ (if blank(name) then [IdentityComponentMissing(o, "tool name")] else []),
    ApprovalIdentity(origin, call_id, name) =>
      (if blank(origin) then [IdentityComponentMissing(o, "approval origin")] else [])
        ++ (if blank(call_id) then [IdentityComponentMissing(o, "approval call_id")] else [])
        ++ (if blank(name) then [IdentityComponentMissing(o, "approval name")] else []),
    ExtensionEffectIdentity(ext_id, class_id, _call_id) =>
      (if blank(ext_id) then [IdentityComponentMissing(o, "extension_effect ext_id")] else [])
        ++ (if blank(class_id) then [IdentityComponentMissing(o, "extension_effect class_id")] else []),
    EnvironmentReadIdentity(key) =>
      if blank(key) then [IdentityComponentMissing(o, "environment_read key")] else [],
    RandomDrawIdentity(bounds) =>
      if blank(bounds) then [IdentityComponentMissing(o, "random_draw bounds")] else [],
    ClockAdvanceIdentity => [],
    -- WI-D17. The path is the class's ONLY component, so a blank one leaves an
    -- interaction that names no file — the shape this rule rejects for every
    -- other single-component class.
    FileWriteIdentity(path) =>
      if blank(path) then [IdentityComponentMissing(o, "file_write path")] else [],
    FileRemoveIdentity(path) =>
      if blank(path) then [IdentityComponentMissing(o, "file_remove path")] else [],
    DirMakeIdentity(path) =>
      if blank(path) then [IdentityComponentMissing(o, "dir_make path")] else []
  }
}

pure func chunk_offsets_monotone(cs: [StreamChunkRecord], last: int) -> bool {
  match cs {
    [] => true,
    c :: rest => if c.offset_ms < last then false else chunk_offsets_monotone(rest, c.offset_ms)
  }
}

pure func any_chunk_negative(cs: [StreamChunkRecord]) -> bool {
  match cs {
    [] => false,
    c :: rest => if c.offset_ms < 0 then true else any_chunk_negative(rest)
  }
}

-- Time, deadlines, streaming and the status/fault pairing, per interaction.
pure func validate_outcome(i: Interaction, known_class_ids: [string]) -> [ProgramRejection] {
  let o = i.identity.ordinal;
  let out = i.outcome;
  let has_deadline = class_has_deadline(i.identity.body);
  (if out.advance_ms < 0 then [NegativeTime(o, "advance_ms", out.advance_ms)] else [])
    ++ (if any_chunk_negative(out.chunks) then [NegativeTime(o, "a stream chunk offset_ms", -1)] else [])
    ++ (if i.deadline_ms < -1 then [NegativeTime(o, "deadline_ms", i.deadline_ms)] else [])
    ++ (if has_deadline && i.deadline_ms < 0 then
          [DeadlineDisagreesWithClass(o, "is a ${interaction_kind(i)} and records no deadline (-1)")]
        else [])
    ++ (if not has_deadline && i.deadline_ms != -1 then
          [DeadlineDisagreesWithClass(o, "is a ${interaction_kind(i)} and records a deadline of ${i.deadline_ms}")]
        else [])
    ++ (if List.length(out.chunks) > 0 && not class_may_stream(i.identity.body) then
          [StreamOnNonProvider(o)] else [])
    ++ (if not chunk_offsets_monotone(out.chunks, 0) then [ChunkOffsetsNotMonotone(o)] else [])
    ++ (match out.status {
          OutcomeFault =>
            if blank(out.fault_class_id) then
              [OutcomeStatusDisagreesWithFault(o, "records a fault with no fault_class_id")]
            else if contains_str(known_class_ids, out.fault_class_id) then []
            else [ImpossibleReference(o, "names fault class '${out.fault_class_id}', which no catalogue row defines")],
          OutcomeOk =>
            if blank(out.fault_class_id) then []
            else [OutcomeStatusDisagreesWithFault(o, "is an ok outcome naming fault class '${out.fault_class_id}'")],
          OutcomeMissing =>
            (if blank(out.fault_class_id) then []
             else [OutcomeStatusDisagreesWithFault(o, "is a missing outcome naming fault class '${out.fault_class_id}'")])
              ++ (match i.identity.body {
                    EnvironmentReadIdentity(_) => [],
                    -- WI-A13 stage 2 WIDENED this rule to the approval class.
                    -- D2 names `value_or_missing` for the environment read, and
                    -- stage 1 read that as "missing is env-only". Stage 2's
                    -- discovery found the second reading, and an exhausted
                    -- approval queue is what forced the choice:
                    --
                    --   A. missing is ENV-ONLY. An approval read that hits
                    --      end-of-input must then record OutcomeOk with an
                    --      empty payload — which makes it indistinguishable
                    --      from an operator who typed an empty line. A12
                    --      created `ApprovalInput.eof` precisely to keep those
                    --      apart ("a recorded world must be able to replay an
                    --      empty line that the operator actually typed as
                    --      distinct from a closed channel", ports.ail), and the
                    --      driver's policy BRANCHES differently on the two. So
                    --      reading A makes a class of run unreplayable.
                    --
                    --   B. missing means THE WORLD HAD NO VALUE TO SERVE, which
                    --      is equally true of an absent env key and a drained
                    --      approval queue.
                    --
                    -- B is taken. It preserves a distinction production already
                    -- depends on, and it keeps OutcomeOk meaning one thing.
                    -- The rule stays CLOSED for every other class: a provider,
                    -- tool, extension-effect, random-draw or clock interaction
                    -- with a 'missing' status is still rejected, because none of
                    -- those has a "the world declined to answer" state that is
                    -- not already a fault class in A7's catalogue.
                    ApprovalIdentity(_, _, _) => [],
                    -- WI-D17 WIDENED IT A SECOND TIME, to `file_remove` ONLY,
                    -- and reading B is what decides it rather than a preference
                    -- for a smaller diff. A remove of a path the world does not
                    -- hold is "the world had no value to serve" in exactly the
                    -- sense an absent env key and a drained approval queue are,
                    -- and the two alternatives are the two reading A already
                    -- lost to:
                    --
                    --   OutcomeOk  — indistinguishable from a remove that
                    --     actually deleted something. `FileMutation.ok` exists
                    --     to keep those apart at the seam (`removeFileResult`
                    --     returns Err for an absent path, measured), so
                    --     collapsing them in the log makes a class of run
                    --     unreplayable in precisely the way `ApprovalInput.eof`
                    --     was created to prevent.
                    --   OutcomeFault — would require a fault_class_id, and A7's
                    --     catalogue declares no filesystem class. Recording one
                    --     would either manufacture a class the catalogue does
                    --     not contain, or leave a fault uncountable by D11's
                    --     class-reached counter, which the arm above rejects.
                    --
                    -- `file_write` is NOT admitted. A write has no "the world
                    -- had nothing to give" state — there is always something to
                    -- write — so its only non-ok outcome is a genuine failure,
                    -- and that belongs to the fault arm whenever the catalogue
                    -- grows a class for it.
                    --
                    -- WI-D18's `dir_make` IS NOT ADMITTED EITHER, and it reaches
                    -- the rejecting arm below by falling through rather than by
                    -- being named — which is why it is stated here. `mkdirAll`
                    -- is IDEMPOTENT: creating a directory that already exists
                    -- succeeds, so "it was already there" is an ordinary `ok`
                    -- and not a missing answer. The class therefore has no
                    -- reading-B state at all, and the widening stays at exactly
                    -- one mutator. `execution_program_dst` mutant 12e is what
                    -- says so rather than this sentence.
                    FileRemoveIdentity(_) => [],
                    _ => [OutcomeStatusDisagreesWithFault(o, "is a ${interaction_kind(i)} with a 'missing' status, which D2 defines only for an environment read, (WI-A13) an approval whose queue is exhausted, and (WI-D17) a file_remove of a path the world does not hold")]
                  })
        })
}

-- An extension effect names a fault class in its IDENTITY as well as possibly
-- in its outcome, and D2 requires that id to be "the same stable identifier
-- D3's catalogue and D11's coverage counters use, so a process-failure class
-- cannot be counted as reached under one name and mapped under another".
pure func validate_static_references(i: Interaction, known_class_ids: [string]) -> [ProgramRejection] {
  match i.identity.body {
    ExtensionEffectIdentity(_, class_id, _) =>
      if blank(class_id) || contains_str(known_class_ids, class_id) then []
      else [ImpossibleReference(i.identity.ordinal,
              "names effect class '${class_id}', which no catalogue row defines; D2 requires the extension-effect class id to be the SAME stable identifier D3's catalogue and D11's counters use")],
    _ => []
  }
}

-- Duplicates. Deliberately blind to the identity body — see design note 2.
pure func duplicate_ordinals(xs: [Interaction], seen: [int]) -> [ProgramRejection] {
  match xs {
    [] => [],
    i :: rest =>
      if contains_int(seen, i.identity.ordinal) then
        [DuplicateOrdinal(i.identity.ordinal)] ++ duplicate_ordinals(rest, seen)
      else duplicate_ordinals(rest, seen ++ [i.identity.ordinal])
  }
}

-- Density. Ordinals are the GLOBAL encounter order, so position n must carry
-- ordinal n: a gap is an interaction dropped from the program, which is exactly
-- the completeness defect that a determinism check cannot see.
pure func dense_ordinals(xs: [Interaction], expected: int) -> [ProgramRejection] {
  match xs {
    [] => [],
    i :: rest =>
      if i.identity.ordinal == expected then dense_ordinals(rest, expected + 1)
      else [OrdinalNotDense(expected, i.identity.ordinal)] ++ dense_ordinals(rest, expected + 1)
  }
}

pure func validate_interactions(xs: [Interaction], known_class_ids: [string]) -> [ProgramRejection] {
  match xs {
    [] => [],
    i :: rest =>
      validate_identity(i.identity)
        ++ validate_outcome(i, known_class_ids)
        ++ validate_static_references(i, known_class_ids)
        ++ validate_interactions(rest, known_class_ids)
  }
}

-- The whole structural gate, in one call.
--
-- `known_class_ids` is an ARGUMENT rather than a call to the catalogue, for the
-- reason A10 established for its derived sets: a validator that consults the
-- live catalogue makes its own reference check a tautology under any edit that
-- changes both together, and it makes this module untestable without one.
export pure func validate_program(p: ExecutionProgram, known_class_ids: [string]) -> [ProgramRejection] {
  validate_schema(p)
    ++ validate_initial_world(p.initial_world)
    ++ (if List.length(p.interactions) == 0 then [EmptyProgram] else [])
    ++ duplicate_ordinals(p.interactions, [])
    ++ dense_ordinals(p.interactions, 0)
    ++ validate_interactions(p.interactions, known_class_ids)
}

export pure func program_ok(p: ExecutionProgram, known_class_ids: [string]) -> bool {
  List.length(validate_program(p, known_class_ids)) == 0
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Declared bounds (D2)
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Separate from the structural validator because D2 classifies a bounds
-- violation differently: "Exceeding a bound is a generator/harness failure, not
-- an unbounded test run" — a statement about the GENERATOR, while the
-- structural rules are statements about the artifact. Keeping them apart is
-- what lets the discovery gate report generator failures as their own count.
export type BoundViolation = { bound: string, limit: int, actual: int }

export pure func bound_violation_message(v: BoundViolation) -> string {
  "[generator-bound-exceeded] ${v.bound} limit is ${v.limit} but the program reached ${v.actual}; D2 makes exceeding a declared bound a generator failure, not an unbounded run"
}

pure func max_chunks(xs: [Interaction], best: int) -> int {
  match xs {
    [] => best,
    i :: rest => {
      let n = List.length(i.outcome.chunks);
      max_chunks(rest, if n > best then n else best)
    }
  }
}

pure func max_payload(xs: [Interaction], best: int) -> int {
  match xs {
    [] => best,
    i :: rest => {
      let n = Str.length(i.outcome.payload);
      max_payload(rest, if n > best then n else best)
    }
  }
}

pure func max_advance(xs: [Interaction], best: int) -> int {
  match xs {
    [] => best,
    i :: rest => max_advance(rest, if i.outcome.advance_ms > best then i.outcome.advance_ms else best)
  }
}

pure func check_bound(name: string, limit: int, actual: int) -> [BoundViolation] {
  if actual > limit then [{ bound: name, limit: limit, actual: actual }] else []
}

export pure func validate_bounds(p: ExecutionProgram) -> [BoundViolation] {
  check_bound("max_interactions", p.bounds.max_interactions, List.length(p.interactions))
    ++ check_bound("max_chunks_per_interaction", p.bounds.max_chunks_per_interaction, max_chunks(p.interactions, 0))
    ++ check_bound("max_payload_bytes", p.bounds.max_payload_bytes, max_payload(p.interactions, 0))
    ++ check_bound("max_clock_advance_ms", p.bounds.max_clock_advance_ms, max_advance(p.interactions, 0))
}

export pure func within_bounds(p: ExecutionProgram) -> bool {
  List.length(validate_bounds(p)) == 0
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rejection-set query helpers, exported for the acceptance script
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

export pure func has_program_rule(rs: [ProgramRejection], rule: string) -> bool {
  match rs {
    [] => false,
    r :: rest => if program_rule(r) == rule then true else has_program_rule(rest, rule)
  }
}

export pure func first_program_message(rs: [ProgramRejection]) -> Option[string] {
  match rs {
    [] => None,
    r :: _ => Some(program_message(r))
  }
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Inline tests. The PROGRAM fixtures live in
-- scripts/dst/execution_program_dst.ail, where a whole program can be mutated
-- one field at a time. What is tested here is the part with no fixture: the
-- derivations on the identity itself.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- D8's fail-closed decode, asserted on the version list rather than on a
-- fixture: an unknown schema is undecodable, and the CURRENT schema is
-- decodable. The second half is the one that matters — a decodable list that
-- omitted the version this build writes would reject every program it produced.
--
-- WI-D22 ADDED THE THIRD AND FOURTH CLAUSES, and the third is the one the first
-- bump needed. Through WI-D21 this list was `[program_schema_version()]`, so the
-- first two clauses were satisfied by CONSTRUCTION and neither could see a bump
-- that dropped the predecessor. The list now has to be two long and has to hold
-- `execution-program/1` by name, which is the migration D8 requires stated as an
-- assertion rather than as a comment in the list's own body.
--
-- The length is pinned as well as the membership. A third version added without
-- a decode path in `dst_persistence` would satisfy "contains v1 and contains the
-- current one" while leaving the new one undecodable in fact, and only the count
-- says so.
pure func test_current_schema_is_decodable_and_an_unknown_one_is_not() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    contains_str(decodable_schema_versions(), program_schema_version())
      && not contains_str(decodable_schema_versions(), "execution-program/0")
      -- WI-D22: the predecessor stays decodable across the bump.
      && contains_str(decodable_schema_versions(), "execution-program/1")
      && List.length(decodable_schema_versions()) == 2
      -- And the bump actually happened: this build does not WRITE v1.
      && program_schema_version() == "execution-program/2"
  }

-- WI-D21. THE `ExtPorts.proc_exec` BRIDGE BUILDS AN INVOCATION THIS VALIDATOR
-- REJECTS, and this row is that sentence executed rather than written down.
--
-- `session.ail`'s `proc_exec` closure constructs
-- `{ call: { id: "", tool: <tool>, … } }` — a BLANK call id, because an
-- extension calling this seam is not answering a model tool call and has none to
-- supply. `ports.recording_tool` then records
-- `ToolIdentity("loop_v2", inv.call.id, inv.call.tool)`. The first argument
-- below is that identity, verbatim.
--
-- Nothing calls the seam today, so the defect is LATENT and this row is what
-- keeps it from being discovered by whoever routes the first caller. It is
-- deliberately NOT repaired here: the repair is the identity-class decision, and
-- both roads out of it are blocked on work this item is scoped to price rather
-- than take —
--
--   * supplying a call id would invent an identity the effect does not have,
--     which is the shape D2's `ExtensionEffectIdentity` exemption exists to
--     avoid; and
--   * moving the class to `ExtensionEffectIdentity` needs an `ext_id` the
--     `ExtPorts` bridge cannot name (one record serves every extension, and
--     `ExtCtx` carries no id), plus an eighth `record_interaction` adapter —
--     no existing one constructs that class.
--
-- So the assertion states the current answer and its cost. The SECOND clause is
-- the control: an ordinary driver-issued dispatch carries a real call id and
-- passes, so this row fails for the blank and not for the shape of the fixture.
pure func test_the_ext_proc_exec_invocation_shape_records_a_rejected_tool_identity() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    let from_the_bridge = validate_identity({
      ordinal: 1,
      body: ToolIdentity("loop_v2", "", "BashExec")
    });
    let from_the_driver = validate_identity({
      ordinal: 1,
      body: ToolIdentity("loop_v2", "call_1", "BashExec")
    });
    has_program_rule(from_the_bridge, "program-identity-component-missing")
      && List.length(from_the_bridge) == 1
      && List.length(from_the_driver) == 0
  }

-- The other half of the same finding: `ExtensionEffectIdentity` IS the class
-- whose call id may be blank, so the shape the bridge produces is legitimate
-- there and rejected under `ToolIdentity`. Asserted so that the identity-class
-- decision rests on a measured rule rather than on a reading of D2's prose.
--
-- THE FIRST CLAUSE USES `extension_effect_fault` DELIBERATELY. A7 declares that
-- class (`dst_fault_catalogue.ail:405`) and its coverage-gap entry names
-- `ExtPorts.proc_exec` by name, so the `class_id` this seam would need is not a
-- gap — it is already written. What the bridge lacks is ONE component, the
-- `ext_id`, and the second clause pins that: blank everything is exactly two
-- rejections, and the call id is not one of them.
pure func test_the_extension_effect_class_exempts_the_call_id_and_lacks_only_the_ext_id() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    let what_the_seam_would_record = validate_identity({
      ordinal: 1,
      body: ExtensionEffectIdentity("compose", "extension_effect_fault", "")
    });
    let what_the_bridge_could_supply_today = validate_identity({
      ordinal: 1,
      body: ExtensionEffectIdentity("", "", "")
    });
    List.length(what_the_seam_would_record) == 0
      && List.length(what_the_bridge_could_supply_today) == 2
  }
